lesson: 188
title: The longest equal pieces
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: To cut two lengths into equal pieces with nothing left, the piece must divide both lengths; the longest such piece is the greatest common divisor.
explain: Ribbons of 24 cm and 36 cm: the numbers that divide both are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12, so the longest piece is 12 cm.
explain: Then 24 cm gives 2 pieces and 36 cm gives 3 pieces: 5 pieces in all.
vars: a=name g=2..12 p=2..9 q=3..9
need: p < q
need: (p % 2 != 0 || q % 2 != 0) && (p % 3 != 0 || q % 3 != 0)
let: x = g * p
let: y = g * q
answer: p + q
exercise: {a} has two ribbons, {x} cm and {y} cm long. {a.He} cuts both into pieces of the same length, as long as possible, with nothing left over. How many pieces does {a.he} get?
exercise: Two ropes are {x} m and {y} m long. They are cut into equal pieces, as long as possible, and no rope is wasted. How many pieces are there in all?
exercise: {a} wants to cut a {x} cm strip and a {y} cm strip of paper into equal strips that are as long as possible, without any paper left. How many strips will {a.he} have?
exercise: A {x} m board and a {y} m board are sawn into pieces of equal length, the longest possible, with no waste. How many pieces are there altogether?
exercise: Two wires, {x} cm and {y} cm long, must be cut into equal parts as long as possible with nothing left. How many parts will there be?
test: {a} cuts a {y} cm ribbon and a {x} cm ribbon so that every piece has the same length and nothing is thrown away. The pieces are as long as they can be. Count the pieces.
test: Without wasting any cloth, a tailor cuts two strips of {x} cm and {y} cm into the fewest equal pieces. How many pieces does the tailor make?
test: Two pipes of {x} m and {y} m are divided into pieces of one length with nothing left, and the pieces are as long as possible. What is the total number of pieces?
exercise: Two pieces of ribbon measure {x} cm and {y} cm. {a} cuts them into pieces all of the same length, the longest length possible, with no ribbon left over. How many pieces are there altogether?
exercise: How many pieces will there be in total if two ropes of {y} m and {x} m are both cut into equal pieces, as long as possible, with none wasted?
exercise: In a workshop in Ontario, two planks {x} dm and {y} dm long are sawn into equal lengths, as long as they can be, with nothing left. How many pieces does that give?
exercise: {a} has {x} cm of red tape and {y} cm of blue tape. {a.He} wants every piece the same length, as long as possible, and no tape left. How many pieces will {a.he} have in all?
exercise: Ribbons of {x} cm and {y} cm; equal pieces, the longest possible, no waste. How many pieces combined?
exercise: A gardener in Kent has two hoses, {x} m and {y} m long, and cuts them into equal lengths, the longest possible, so that nothing is left over. How many lengths does the gardener get in total?
exercise: Two strips of fabric, {x.words} centimetres and {y.words} centimetres long, are cut into the longest equal pieces with no fabric left. How many pieces are made?
exercise: {a} wants to cut two sticks, {y} cm and {x} cm long, into pieces of one length, as long as possible, leaving nothing. How many pieces is that together?
test: What is the total number of pieces when two cables, {x} m and {y} m long, are cut into the longest pieces of equal length without any cable left?
test: A baker in Melbourne rolls two strips of dough, {y} cm and {x} cm long, and cuts them into equal bits, each as long as it can be, with no dough left. How many bits are there altogether?
test: {a} has to share two lengths of string, {x} cm and {y} cm, into equal pieces that are as long as possible and leave no string over. How many pieces of string will there be combined?
test: Two logs of {x} dm and {y} dm are chopped into pieces that all match in length, the longest possible, and no wood is left. How many pieces are chopped in total?
test: The longest equal pieces with nothing left over are cut from two ribbons of {x} cm and {y} cm. Altogether, how many pieces?
test: In a school in Wellington, a teacher cuts two rolls of paper, {x} cm and {y} cm long, into equal sheets, each as long as possible, leaving no paper. How many sheets are there together?
