exam: Two ribbons, of lengths {x} cm and {y} cm respectively, are to be divided into pieces of uniform length, such length being the greatest possible and no remnant remaining. Determine the total number of pieces obtained.
exam: It is given that two ropes of {x} metres and {y} metres are to be cut entirely into equal pieces of the greatest admissible length. Ascertain the aggregate number of pieces so produced.
exam: Whereas {a} is required to divide two planks, measuring {x} dm and {y} dm, into segments of identical length, the said length to be maximal and no material to be wasted, state the number of segments obtained in total.
exam: Determine the number of pieces resulting from the division, without remainder, of two lengths of cable, of {x} metres and {y} metres, into pieces all of one length, that length being the greatest consistent with the said conditions.
exercise: {a} has ribbons of {x} cm and {y} cm, and a third one, {x+y} cm, that 'fell off the back of a lorry' — Mum made {a.him} give it back. {a} cuts the ribbons that are left into equal pieces, as long as possible, with nothing left over. How many pieces does {a.he} get?
exercise: {a.His} brother was economical with the truth: he said the two ropes were {x+1} m and {y} m. They are really {x} m and {y} m. {a} cuts them into equal pieces, as long as possible, with no rope wasted. How many pieces are there?
